From b26a4ebc9a6c460f10e9a8bbbbb2ffd9aff9c1aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emjupy <dev@example.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 18:20:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Tint output by kind, and stop rules running past the right
 edge

Output is no longer one flat band. Each piece is tinted for what it is:
red for a traceback, yellow for stderr that is not one, white for a
figure, the ordinary accent for everything else. stderr counts as a
warning because that is where warnings.warn, logging and progress bars
go; images default to white because a matplotlib figure is white and a
tinted band around it reads as a frame the plot does not have.

The tints are text properties on each piece rather than one overlay
across the whole box, so a cell holding a figure, a warning and a
traceback shows all three correctly -- an overlay face would have
overridden every one of them. Each face still sets only :background and
:extend, so font-lock colours on tracebacks show through.

All four are customizable: emjupy-output-color,
emjupy-output-error-color, emjupy-output-warning-color and
emjupy-output-image-color each take a colour, 'auto, or nil to fall back
to the ordinary output colour. 'auto derives from the theme and works on
light and dark alike; emjupy-output-tint-blend controls how loud the red
and yellow are.

Three separate causes made rules run past the right edge, all fixed:
window-body-width counts the line-number column, which is drawn inside
the text area, so with display-line-numbers-mode a rule overshot by the
width of the numbers; a notebook is rendered before it is displayed, so
it was measured against no window and fell back to a hardcoded 100
columns; and Emacs cannot always report exactly how many columns are
usable, so emjupy-box-right-margin (default 2) leaves a couple spare and
is the knob for setups that still need more.
